# Maybe

The implementation source is `planar-shared/src/maybe.ts`.
Import `Maybe`, `maybe`, `nothing`, `isNothing`, `maybeMap`, `optional`, and `just` from `@planar/shared`.

## Preference and Scope

- This is a soft preference when designing project-owned optional values such as lookup misses, parsed optional fields, and domain optionals.
- Apply it to new signatures and files already being changed; do not perform a repository-wide migration.
- When choosing between `foo?: T` and Maybe for project-owned data, prefer `foo?: Maybe<T>` or `foo: Maybe<T>`.
- Do not edit generated or ignored files for this rule.

## Record Shapes

- Sparse records may use `field?: Maybe<T>`; omit unused keys instead of filling them with `undefined`.
- Complete or parsed records use `field: Maybe<T>` when the key is always present.

## Boundaries

- Keep native `null` and `undefined` in React, DOM, Node and other platform APIs, JSON/fetch wire formats, Zod schemas, and generated Swagger/client code.
- Do not rewrite those boundary, platform, schema, wire, or generated types to Maybe.
- Convert optional values entering project-owned domain code with `maybe(value)`.

## Leaving Maybe

```typescript
// ❌
const name: string | null | undefined = row.name;
const label = name || 'unknown';
const id = actor.id!;

// ✅
const name: Maybe<string> = maybe(row.name);
const label = optional(name, 'unknown');
const id = just(actor.id);
```

- Use `just(x)` only when absence violates an invariant; it fails fast with `Null reference exception`.
- When absence is allowed, use an `isNothing(x)` branch, `maybeMap(x, f)`, or `optional(x, default)`.
- Do not use `x || default` on Maybe because it drops `0`, `false`, and `''`.
- Do not expand this into a functional-programming framework: no new helpers, `bind`, `Result`, fp-ts, or similar abstractions unless the user asks.
